and $K_2=\{a\to b\}$, with ordinary propositional
consequence. Then $b\in\operatorname{Cn}(K_1\cup K_2)$ but
$b\notin\operatorname{Cn}(K_1)$ and
$b\notin\operatorname{Cn}(K_2)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Modus ponens yields $b$ jointly. A model with $a$ true and $b$
false satisfies $K_1$; a model with both false satisfies $K_2$.
Thus neither collection separately entails $b$.\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
This is an example of \emph{distributed knowledge synergy}, not a
new theorem relative to the full frozen premises, which may already
entail $b$. It also differs from a multiarticle transcendent theorem,
which requires non-derivability from each \emph{full individual
closure}. The distinction must not be blurred.\end{remark}


\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
